<% content_for :title, "After Drinking Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page">

  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS", toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / AFTER DRINKING
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>I DRANK.</span>
        <span>WHAT THE HELL</span>
        <span>IS NEXT?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        MY PLAN FOR WHAT HAPPENS NOW.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / RIGHT NOW -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <p class="relapse-plan-label">01 / RIGHT NOW</p>

      <h2>START WITH<br>WHAT’S HAPPENING.</h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">
        <p>
          So, you drank. The sky isn't literally falling, but you're
          not doing very well. You're probably scared out of your mind,
          ashamed, angry, pissed off, and already telling yourself that
          you might as well keep going since you've started and messed
          up. What's one more? Well, one more means two, and two means
          five. Stop if you haven't. You don't have to figure out your
          entire recovery right now, but just start with what needs your
          attention: your safety, how you're feeling, and where you can
          get help.
        </p>

        <p>
          Be honest with yourself about your situation: why are you
          drinking? What are the circumstances and what are you feeling?
          Grab your journal, a blanket, and a bottle of water, and settle
          in to rest and figure things out. The drinking isn't the
          problem, it's why you did it. Call someone too and just be
          honest, and then take it from there. This is a speedbump in
          your recovery, it's not a definition of who you are as a person.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / IMMEDIATE SAFETY -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <p class="relapse-plan-label">02 / IMMEDIATE SAFETY</p>

      <h2>WHAT NEEDS<br>ATTENTION FIRST?</h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>MY IMMEDIATE SAFETY STEPS</h3>

          <p>
            First, figure out where you are and whether you're safe.
            Don't drive. Call a sober person who can help you arrange
            a safe ride or stay with you. Tell them what you drank,
            roughly how much, when, and whether you took anything else.
            Give them the actual information, not the version that
            sounds less embarrassing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Journaling can wait if you're too intoxicated to take care
            of yourself. A blanket and water are comforts, not treatment
            for alcohol poisoning. Don't assume you can just sleep it off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>MEDICAL &amp; URGENT SUPPORT</h3>

          <p>
            Call 911 for suspected alcohol poisoning, including difficulty
            waking up, slow or irregular breathing, seizures, or severe
            confusion. Don't wait for every symptom to appear. If you're
            with someone showing these signs, stay with them until help arrives.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you've been drinking heavily and regularly, or have a
            history of alcohol withdrawal, get medical advice about
            stopping safely. Withdrawal can be dangerous. Ask a clinician
            or pharmacist about medication questions instead of guessing
            about doses or interactions.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / BRING SOMEONE IN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <p class="relapse-plan-label">03 / BRING SOMEONE IN</p>

      <h2>MAKE THE<br>NEXT CONTACT.</h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>WHO I WILL TELL</h3>

          <p>
            Pick someone who can help you with what's happening now:
            your sponsor, a sober friend, a family member, or someone
            from your psych. You don't need to give a speech
            explaining every decision that got you here. Tell them
            you drank, where you are, and what help you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be specific. Do you need someone to stay on the phone,
            help you get home, come sit with you, or help you contact
            your treatment provider? Ask for that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>MY BACKUP</h3>

          <p>
            If the first person doesn't answer, try someone else.
            An unanswered call isn't a verdict on whether people care
            about you. They might be working, sleeping, or away from
            their phone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Have another person or your treatment program's support
            contact ready. If the situation is medically urgent,
            contact emergency services rather than waiting for
            a friend to call back.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="relapse-plan-script">
        <h3>THE MESSAGE</h3>

        <p>
          "Hey. I drank, and I'm embarrassed, but I don't want to hide it
          or keep going. I'm at [location]. Can you call me and help me
          figure out my next step? I need [a safe ride / someone to sit
          with me / help contacting my psych]."
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / THE NEXT STRETCH -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <p class="relapse-plan-label">04 / THE NEXT STRETCH</p>

      <h2>WHAT HAPPENS<br>AFTER THIS MOMENT?</h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>MY NEXT STEPS</h3>

          <p>
            Once immediate safety and medical needs are addressed,
            make a plan for the next few hours with someone you trust.
            Where will you be? Who will check in? What needs to happen
            so you're not sitting alone with alcohol and a growing
            pile of shame?
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the plan concrete. You don't need ten new promises
            about the rest of your life. You need to know what you're
            doing next and who knows what's going on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>MY FOLLOW-UP SUPPORT</h3>

          <p>
            Tell your treatment provider or recovery support what
            happened. If you have a therapist, prescriber, sponsor,
            or recovery group, decide who you're contacting and when.
            Put it in your phone instead of leaving it as something
            you'll deal with "later."
          </p>

          <p>
            Include anything else that's been going on: changes in
            your mood, sleep, pain, routines, or medications. You don't
            have to know how all of it fits together before you bring
            it up.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / LOOKING BACK LATER -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">
      <p class="relapse-plan-label">05 / LOOKING BACK LATER</p>

      <h2>WHAT CAN<br>I LEARN HERE?</h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>WHAT LED UP TO IT</h3>

          <p>
            When you're safe and ready, look at the lead-up. What was
            happening that day? What had been happening all week?
            What were you feeling, avoiding, wanting, or telling yourself?
            When did drinking start to seem like an option?
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for specifics instead of landing on "because I'm
            a fucking idiot." That doesn't give you anything useful
            to work with. A missed appointment, an argument, a pain
            flare, or a thought you kept entertaining gives you
            something you can actually address.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">
          <h3>WHAT MY PLAN NEEDS NOW</h3>

          <p>
            Where did your old plan stop being useful? Was a contact
            unavailable? Did you notice a warning sign but not know
            what to do with it? Were you relying on an activity you
            couldn't manage that day?
          </p>

          <p>
            Add what was missing with your support team. Make the
            next step more specific, add a backup, or bring a problem
            to someone who can help. You can take responsibility for
            drinking without making shame your entire recovery plan.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>